WEBVTT

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:31.220
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place. I'm Chelsea Wills. In this fifth season, we're speaking with visionary chefs, gardeners, farmers, organizers, artists, and scientists about migrations of all kinds. We'll hear about food and the experience of leaving home and in finding new ones, of decolonizing food traditions and tracing recipes through the movements of diaspora.

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:47.900
 Delicious Revolution is made by Devon Samson and Chelsea Wills. You can subscribe to the show on Apple Podcasts or wherever you get your podcasts. And you can find all of our episodes along with pictures and more on the website deliciousrevolutionshow.com.

00:00:48.380 --> 00:01:11.530
 Well, welcome to Delicious Revolution, Severine. And I wanted to start just talking to you a little bit. There's so many projects that you have started and you're involved in, but I want to start with the Greenhorns and ask you where you were and how that idea first came about.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:32.980
 Well, it's funny because looking back at the kind of genesis moment of Greenhorns, it really was such a different time in the world and such a different time kind of between my ears, like the atmosphere of my analysis, my own personal analysis of how to make a change in the world and where to put energy and effort.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:02:00.550
 But the basic premise has always been that hands on the land, people starting farms, young people entering organic agriculture has something to do with policy and something to do with markets, but it also has something to do with personal narrative and personal ambition and courage and teamwork and the spirit, you know, the drive.

00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:06.750
 And that we could potentially help people who are wanting to get on the land.

00:02:06.950 --> 00:02:18.260
 Through cultural means and through encouragement and through, you know, public programming and education and guidebooks and kind of orientation on that journey.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:33.840
 And so every single thing that we have done with Greenhorns has been in some way informed by what we observed in our client audience or in our constituency, which is young farmers, you know, what did they need to get on the land?

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.920
 What other encouragement, you know, what would make a difference to people?

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:44.750
 So because if you look at the different forms of that's taken, it's, you know, very polymorphic or you'd say transmedia kind of work.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:50.530
 But the core is always the same as, you know, what do you need to get on onto the land?

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:51.940
 And how can we help?

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.200
 You know, how can I be how can we be helpful to you?

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.250
 And so when was it that you started Greenhorns and when where were you?

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:07.430
 Well, we at that time I had a little office in Berkeley in order to start doing Greenhorns.

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:08.330
 Well, let's see.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:12.180
 Did I have the office yet or I just I started it and then I got the office?

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:14.970
 Well, actually, I was living out of the office for a while.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.910
 But I think that's a Bay Area tradition.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.690
 Yeah, well, we did it a few times.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:30.580
 I was making a film series at Berkeley that was following a lecture series.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.100
 The lecture series was all about careers in sustainable agriculture for women.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:49.200
 So we had women winemakers, we had women ethnobotanists, we had women, you know, policy advocates, all different women in the Bay Area who had made a kind of career of sustainable agriculture and kind of evening sessions.

00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:50.860
 And we had so many people come.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:57.450
 And so I was at UC Berkeley getting a degree in conservation with a focus in agroecology.

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:07.220
 And at that time, University of California, Berkeley was receiving a lot of money from British Petroleum and Novartis.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:16.930
 And there was a kind of a student, you know, uprising saying, you know, we're not interested in the corporatization of our of our research agenda of this university.

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.410
 And, you know, we see our role differently.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:26.210
 And, you know, so there was a conversation about the direction of travel for the students and for the school.

00:04:26.210 --> 00:04:34.360
 And so we really kind of found ourselves in a like a profession, an alternative professional development track at that time.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.620
 And so and, you know, had a good reception.

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:41.500
 So then built a little mailing list and then started organizing films.

00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:50.040
 And I managed to convince Michael Pollan to speak on a film opening of the King Corn.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:55.800
 And then because he was going to speak, then I could get those filmmakers to come out.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:03.930
 And then once they were coming out, I could rent a car and we could go off and, you know, shoot for a few days to make a trailer.

00:05:03.930 --> 00:05:11.530
 So it was very much thanks to the platform of the university and the audience of kind of the malcontents.

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:29.180
 And that particular Michael Pollan moment, to be honest, where so many people were at a certain literacy level and in a fervor about this Obama spring and the chance to enact a better food policy, better farm policy.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:32.470
 So very hopeful moment.

00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:36.720
 Well, that must have been about what, 2007, 2008?

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:37.340
 2008.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:38.100
 Yeah.

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:38.600
 Okay.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:42.760
 I mean, we even had a big party with the Slow Food, you know, festival.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:47.000
 And it was like Wild Boar and, you know, Sandor Katz came.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.180
 And, you know, we were actually watching Obama's inauguration.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.120
 Was it inauguration?

00:05:54.180 --> 00:05:57.330
 Anyway, we were live streaming it at the party.

00:05:58.290 --> 00:06:02.990
 I found some, I found a whole, you know, you find a little cache of old photos from that.

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:03.500
 Right.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:07.120
 But anyway, it was a different moment than the moment now.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:07.440
 For sure, yeah.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.940
 And I think that's the point that I'm trying to do with it.

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:11.320
 Totally.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.320
 So, oh man, it's a different moment in so many ways.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:21.550
 But in terms of young people being farmers, how is it different?

00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.170
 I should say it's like, it's a different moment.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:31.840
 But I guess mostly I would say that because I think, you know, I think we did a lot of very good things in Greenhorns.

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:35.920
 And I think it's been a really wonderful journey and so many smart people have participated.

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:47.090
 But I feel like the bottom of it all was very good timing and luck and being, you know, at the right place in the right time with the right graphics and a microphone.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:58.180
 And so I guess that's just me, you know, kind of 10 years in, you know, nine and a half years in saying, you know, it's not like we were geniuses.

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:00.380
 I think we were just had good timing.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:08.970
 And just as a way of encouraging other people, you know, who are interested in doing good stuff.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:10.060
 So, okay.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:11.040
 So what was the second question?

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:11.780
 The second question was?

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:12.480
 So, yeah.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.580
 So what's changed now?

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:16.980
 And I guess I mean, like, are the challenges different for people?

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.980
 It seems like young farms have popped up all over the place.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:23.150
 But what are the struggles now?

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:23.670
 How have they evolved?

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.540
 Yeah, young farmers have definitely popped up all over the place.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:34.520
 And, you know, so on one hand, you could say in some places the CSA market is oversaturated.

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.660
 You could also say that the organic market has really grown.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:40.780
 Now we're in this moment of Amazon buying Whole Foods.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:51.270
 So you have on one hand, you know, consolidation and gigantism and, you know, kind of monopoly tactics at the retail level.

00:07:51.390 --> 00:07:58.850
 On the other hand, you know, you're likely to have a reaction and you're likely to have more people saying, you know what, I'm going to go and commit to my co-op at this time.

00:07:58.850 --> 00:08:01.450
 Or I'm going to start another, my own co-op.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:09.000
 Or, you know, improvise an alternative route to market for the food because the writing is just so much on the wall.

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:24.930
 And, of course, it's very hard to make general statements about the United States of America, which is why we have the wisdom of the founding fathers, who were most of them farmers, was that states should administrate agriculture.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:33.990
 And that's to do with the highly heterogeneous geography that we are blessed with as a provisionally as a nation.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:38.030
 And so I think it's dangerous to make blanket statements overall.

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:45.400
 However, you can say that some things have gotten saturated, but also the market has really opened up.

00:08:45.400 --> 00:09:03.430
 And so where maybe you used to have, you know, only a few shapes of operation that could really be viable in a certain area, you know, say, take, make a circle around Buffalo, New York, or make a circle around Cleveland and Ohio, or make a circle around any, you know, smaller American city.

00:09:04.450 --> 00:09:22.720
 And, you know, those markets have evolved and those communities have evolved such that there can be now pastured poultry, pastured pork, you know, small fruit, brewing, making, cheesemaking, all the different parts of the food system are, you know, chugging along.

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:32.270
 So it's a more complex ecosystem of enterprises, I would say, with every year that passes.

00:09:33.950 --> 00:09:52.340
 And I think also that in the last 10 years, you know, we used to get some pushback from the not so young farmers saying, why are you only for the young farmers, you know, and that generation who came in during the Back to the Land or who were, you know, slightly after that in the 70s.

00:09:52.640 --> 00:10:03.130
 And it's in the last 10 years, I think a lot of them have also felt that they're, you know, have grown into their age a little and realize, oh, yeah, this is part of the process.

00:10:03.130 --> 00:10:19.530
 And so that there's the kind of sentiment that I receive is, wow, I'm glad to see this young leadership, I'm glad to see this new energy, you know, I'm happy to start passing over responsibilities on these, you know, committees, et cetera, et cetera.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:29.160
 So it's almost like the gravitational pull between the generations that's, like, realigned somehow.

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:37.020
 And, you know, we don't have to carve out our own territory as much to say, well, you know, where do you want to enter agriculture?

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:42.840
 And we're young, we have our own distinct energy and culture and perspective, you know, which we do.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:49.300
 But it feels like the integration is happening very organically.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:51.360
 You know, this is a human phenomenon.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.820
 Yeah, there's been some, I don't know, some integration of those movements.

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:57.840
 I feel like there has been.

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:07.900
 I feel like I think that and also there was a surge in young farmers, like there was a major surge in terms of apprenticeship, you know, applicants and in terms of attendance at conferences.

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:17.100
 And, you know, it just starts to be unavoidable to notice, my God, look at all these people who want in and who are really willing to learn and really willing to work.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:22.910
 And I think that impressed the organic movement in a way that was very positive.

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:24.140
 Right.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:27.080
 You know, like, gosh, you know, maybe we can pull this off.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:36.540
 There's all this new energy coming in, which is, you know, exactly the right feeling to have when you're only 1% of the terrain of the, you know, the territory of the United States.

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:40.950
 Only 1% is organic, even if the market is growing the fastest.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:49.430
 And even if, you know, the literati, the glitterati, the technorati and all the startups all want to eat organic, it's still the minority of our land.

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:51.650
 So, we better be optimistic.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:53.330
 Right, right.

00:11:54.860 --> 00:12:02.210
 Oh, wait, now I'm interested in that link between the kind of back to the land movement of the 70s and this resurgence of young people farming.

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:04.970
 You know, my mom was part of that.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.350
 She was part of an offshoot of the farm.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:11.110
 And do you think the motivations are the same?

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:22.470
 Do you think the reason that it feels important to go back to the farming or for young folks and especially a lot of young folks from urban areas or with fancy educations wanting to farm?

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:26.150
 Well, I've become less and less interested in that narrative.

00:12:26.270 --> 00:12:41.870
 I feel like that is a narrative that sells really well, you know, to say, oh, somebody who could have gone into doctoring or lawyering has gone instead into laying the foundation for a shed, you know, that that's, it's a story in high relief that plays well in the media.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:59.420
 Yeah, you know, I'm much more interested in the connection that are being drawn now to nonviolence, to, you know, especially populations who have been in an incarceration channel.

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:15.290
 You know, we're talking about, we're talking about black food movements, we're talking about food justice, we're talking about, you know, urban food security and food sovereignty and the discourse that's evolving in places that,

00:13:15.460 --> 00:13:18.840
 you know, might seem quite unlikely and a romance.

00:13:19.020 --> 00:13:29.810
 I mean, I think there's this settler narrative of getting, you know, slapping your suspenders against your chest and going out on the prairie and claiming your land and setting up your homestead.

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:37.560
 There's echoes of that that can be very appealing and, you know, self-determination can be very appealing.

00:13:38.000 --> 00:13:45.860
 But, you know, more interesting to me is kind of this, the scar tissue or like, I call it the scar commons.

00:13:46.040 --> 00:13:57.880
 And I've been reading a lot of Peter Linebaugh talking about this, these new phenomena of, of making new spaces that were not possible within the old systems.

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:14.170
 So place, you know, so let me give some concrete examples, reclaiming urban lands and peri-urban lands, partnerships between, you know, food justice projects and community land trusts in blighted urban areas.

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:19.040
 I'm thinking about Southside community land trust in Rhode Island.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:25.500
 I'm thinking about the Cincinnati nuns in Iowa who are working to make their land accessible.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:34.640
 Lots and lots of church lands are slowly becoming activated for community food production.

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:43.280
 Just in the whole, like, Standing Rock response, you know, it was incredible to see where all the food was coming from.

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:53.570
 You know, this just incredible underground food movement moving food into the most inaccessible place in the country, you know, voluntarily, spontaneously, without coordination.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:58.820
 From all over, you know, and just so many native foods and wild harvested food, et cetera.

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:18.090
 So I feel like there's, you know, an amazing anthropology to be done about these different kind of cross-sectoral collaborations that are, you know, opportunistic and spontaneous and to do with making, you know, making do and making things happen.

00:15:18.190 --> 00:15:21.960
 I mean, amazing refugee farms starting up, you know, because of sheer need.

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:30.950
 And I think that as we look at the kind of big mass of land transfer, which is, you know, the mountain I'm hacking at right now is land transfer.

00:15:31.920 --> 00:15:41.590
 And as you look at the numbers and the economics of land succession, you know, it's dismal.

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:43.930
 It's dismal because it's impossible.

00:15:44.150 --> 00:15:56.940
 You know, you could say, well, consolidation at the top and financialization, you know, development pressure, speculation, you know, all these trends, price per acre going up and up and up.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:05.490
 And yet, in the face of this, you know, seemingly futile situation, people are figuring out ways around it.

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:16.470
 And so it's like, that's something I wish I had, you know, another three years with a video camera to go around and document is all the ways that people are.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:29.560
 It's not subverting the mega narrative, but it's quietly figuring, negotiating the edge and quietly finding a solution that can work.

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.910
 There's a creativity to it, isn't there?

00:16:33.450 --> 00:16:33.730
 Yeah.

00:16:33.770 --> 00:16:39.130
 Or you could say creativity or you could say biological destiny or I don't know.

00:16:39.450 --> 00:16:43.490
 It depends on where you're, it depends on, yeah, how you, what, what lenses you're looking at it through.

00:16:44.370 --> 00:16:44.970
 Yeah.

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:52.280
 You mentioned that there's a legal aspect and there's a economic aspect, but you, but you work a lot.

00:16:52.400 --> 00:17:01.240
 And definitely I'm looking through this list of projects to ask you about, but, and there's a lot of work on those fronts, but there's also work on the cultural part of it.

00:17:01.240 --> 00:17:24.080
 Yeah, well, let me go into that because I think, well, so for instance, with our, our work with Agrarian Trust is about access to land and it's about succession of land stewardship, meaning making sure that land that is organically farmed to create, you know, food for local consumption continues to be farmed organically for local food consumption.

00:17:24.080 --> 00:17:29.770
 That we, you know, that we, you know, that we, you know, increase our acreage and increase our self-reliance and increase our regional food economies.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:32.850
 So that's the kind of basic purpose of Agrarian Trust.

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:50.400
 And then, of course, that breaks down into a whole lot of transactional work, you know, literacy on leasing, helping people do role-playing around transferring, you know, figuring out estate plans, figuring out credit, et cetera, et cetera.

00:17:50.990 --> 00:18:02.210
 And so that's a kind of a transactional aspect, you know, legally, I'm pretty interested in this long lineage of thought that says, well, land isn't a commodity.

00:18:02.210 --> 00:18:06.040
 Land, you know, isn't created by, by man or woman.

00:18:06.220 --> 00:18:09.860
 It's, you know, it's the prerequisite for law of life on earth.

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:27.820
 And could we evolve our thinking to recognize it as community to which we belong and not an indenture, which we, to which we become either enslaved or enslaved, which we either, you know, either through rents or through debts.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:46.820
 And so that's a long lineage of thought that is transmitted, you know, ping pong around the planet from Tolstoy to Gandhi to, you know, a bunch of war resistors who created the Community Land Trust, which is right now in the kind of most functional form of that interpretation.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:52.000
 And the Community Land Trust says, well, we hold the land as a community and community ownership.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:19:03.380
 And then the use of that land, the stewardship of that land, the care of that land, the use of that land is assigned to, you know, a member of our community or a tenant or a farmer.

00:19:03.640 --> 00:19:08.790
 And then that person has a responsibility to the whole, to the land.

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:24.190
 So they don't have the right anymore to destroy the land or export the land for sod or, you know, they can't mine it for sand or whatever, because those would be goals not consistent with the goals of the community.

00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:44.160
 So part of the work of Agrarian Trust has been to codify legally, in legal terms, you know, what would be those rights that you would have and what would be the responsibilities that you would have and what are the ways that we would administrate, like votes or the views of the cooperative finance vessel that would hold the land.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:53.060
 Which is all very fun and theoretical and, you know, abstracted and, you know, from someone like me, delightful.

00:19:53.630 --> 00:19:57.980
 And then, of course, once we put it into practice, it's all going to have to keep evolving.

00:19:58.060 --> 00:20:15.390
 And it's almost like we're, you're creating an organization to do the work that has been abandoned in terms of community rights or community responsibilities, because we've had such an episode of private rights and private responsibilities.

00:20:16.150 --> 00:20:16.270
 Right.

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:26.310
 So that's the kind of third part, which is how do we, how do we identify our, as our, as a community, do you know, how much are we the product?

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:34.550
 We, white, we, we, all we, we, you know, women, we, like whichever we you're talking about, you know, there's a group identity there.

00:20:34.940 --> 00:20:40.220
 And I think figuring out in which we you are relating is, is really instructive.

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:49.420
 Like, um, I've just been in Europe attending this conference of organizations that work across Europe.

00:20:49.420 --> 00:21:06.010
 They're like literally a decade ahead than us in the U S and there's been, you know, interesting kind of conflicts that arose with, um, some of the, you know, mostly male, um, French, uh, like peasant union.

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:19.870
 There's the, there's the big, there's the big agribusiness union, and then there's the peasant union, but there's a certain, there was this thing about, well, you know, if the community says we can only farm organically, or the community says we have to do these environmental practices.

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:32.830
 So that's removing our rights and it's our right to decide what we do, what we do on our land, you know, right in there, you can feel this like an unexamined or, uh, there's work there.

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:40.010
 That's so that's like part of the work is like, who is, you know, who are you and who am I and who are we and you know, what are we in together?

00:21:41.150 --> 00:21:48.470
 And, um, you know, and, you know, I want the, I want to do what I want in my, well, in, you know, in my land, it's like, well, can you do what you want in your kitchen?

00:21:49.200 --> 00:22:15.980
 You know, probably not. You probably have to negotiate with your wife. So I think it's that question of codifying legally some of these, because of course, remember capitalism and our legal system evolved to protect the, um, production of commodities and the production of wealth and value through our economic systems, externalizing, you know, such fundamental institutions as motherhood.

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:35.580
 And so if we're talking now also about, you know, the kind of stewardship of the earth that, you know, resembles motherhood in terms of its responsibility and attunedness, you know, we not only have to grow new legal language to do that work, we also have to, you know, punch through a lot of colonial trauma.

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:47.880
 For sure. Yeah. Yeah. And then you, you kind of experiment with the legal structures that, that let you do that. Right. Cause it's, it's a legal structure, not made for common property rights.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:23:09.630
 Yeah. I mean, it's almost like, you know, so much of the work of Eleanor Orstrom, which, um, is the inspiration for our structures and her work is in called, you know, governing the common. So she went around the world studying these different commons based land administration schemes.

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:40.460
 So alpine schemes, you know, rice gardens, you know, rice irrigation districts, the acequia is obviously one of them. That's why we did the whole symposium on the acequia system of the Southwest, um, fisheries, river-based, especially river-based fisheries. So all, she went around and just, you know, tracked what were the relationships and the administrations and the punishments and the sanctions and the, um, rituals that surrounded these successful, durable, land-based, commons-based,

00:23:40.480 --> 00:24:06.690
 natural resource systems. And, um, so those are very interesting as a kind of an economized anthropology of functional human ecological relationships. Now, this next period of our history is, it's almost that there's another kind of future, future anthropology project, um, where you're not now trying to defend, for instance,

00:24:06.690 --> 00:24:38.230
 the customary rights of African or Kenyan villagers against the rationalization that their government is imposing, which is meant to, you know, expedite foreign investment, which is basically land grabbing, you know, so you have a whole, like, set of lawyers going out to try and codify and make legal the customary rights of these African peasants. So that's like the defense work, but the kind of like future work is observing, documenting,

00:24:39.070 --> 00:25:03.160
 you know, articulating, modifying, emergent systems of land commoning. Um, and so, you know, I think agrarian trust is kind of like a petri dish of that because we're so committed to open source and we're so committed to experience sharing and we're so like incredibly happy about navel, myself and myself, especially navel gazing and, you know, uh, intellectualizing the whole thing.

00:25:03.160 --> 00:25:14.290
 Um, but, you know, one particularly interesting frontier of that for me is in the larger project of land repair, you know, so let me just set up a like case study.

00:25:14.990 --> 00:25:15.430
 Sure. Yeah.

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:28.750
 So right now millions, millions, literally millions of, uh, North Africans are migrating across the Mediterranean into Europe because of economic privation and because of all sorts of factors.

00:25:28.750 --> 00:25:41.510
 And, um, you know, it's a question of where do they, you know, how or where do they fit, um, into that small, you know, very small place.

00:25:41.510 --> 00:26:03.230
 You know, could you imagine a circumstance where places that were degraded or deforested or eroded, um, or, you know, over salinated like land that has been destroyed by industrial agriculture would become available resettlement by people who were prepared to restore it.

00:26:03.230 --> 00:26:20.570
 You know, you know, a less extreme example would be all these little villages that are abandoned in the mountain regions in Spain and France, et cetera, you know, people who might otherwise have squatted in the city go off and, uh, squad or cheaply buy these, um, these villages and they, you know, restore them.

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:41.440
 And so there's this whole, you know, open question and, you know, many, many, many sites of experimentation and navigating around this question of essentially new customary rights, emergent rights, um, that are predicated on restoration and ecological repair.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:54.320
 And, um, you know, I'm getting into the abstract and I'm getting into some kind of like theoretical futures, but I don't think it's theoretical because actually there's a lot of examples of this happening spontaneously.

00:26:54.760 --> 00:27:01.660
 But that's a very exciting framework for, you know, to kind of chart and, and track.

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:16.300
 And I wish that we could find an Eleanor Orstrom, you know, around these days who would come along and like find, we would find these sites and we would track them and we would document, you know, what the phenomenology of it is.

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:23.910
 I think we're going to have to learn some new behaviors because we're, we're in a different context than a frontier context.

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:24.610
 Right.

00:27:24.610 --> 00:27:28.770
 You know, land is really limited now for this large humanity.

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:29.670
 Right.

00:27:30.430 --> 00:27:35.610
 Um, so anyway, I'm getting, uh, no, no, no, I, no, I think you're right on.

00:27:35.690 --> 00:27:52.460
 I mean, we're, we're, this whole season, we've kind of stretched around this idea of migrations and, um, because there are these twin trends of displacement of a lot of people for a lot of reasons and kind of the depopulation of rural places in other parts of the world.

00:27:52.960 --> 00:27:53.610
 Yeah.

00:27:54.130 --> 00:27:59.500
 You know, and well, I think we have to be in cahoots with the researchers, number one.

00:28:00.380 --> 00:28:12.840
 And I think also we have got to, um, recognize that, you know, we are part of nature and our reaction to these trends and to these, you know, epiphenomena is, is worth studying.

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:16.420
 You know, that the story about the nation state and the story about the macroeconomy.

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:16.860
 Yeah.

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:17.720
 That's one story.

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:18.240
 Right.

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:29.030
 But there's, you know, there's a huge biological understory going on or overstory, you know, the climactic overstory and the, um, you know, human, human migration understory.

00:28:29.030 --> 00:28:45.090
 And then the kind of biggest understory of all is the degradation of our ecosystems on a map on an unprecedented and massive scale, you know, and the, the, the work of our lifetime is get out there on the land and be part of the solution.

00:28:45.090 --> 00:28:48.640
 And period, like that is all that counts in this time.

00:28:48.640 --> 00:29:16.570
 And so, you know, that, that's, what's so powerful about the young farmers movement is you have a bunch of people saying, you know, I am tuned in and paying attention to that understory, which compels me to perform these tasks, you know, outside in the sun, on land, um, in a way that is legible, not just to the marketplace or to my friends, you know, as cool or rallied or, or, or valuable, um, but is legible to the earth.

00:29:16.650 --> 00:29:18.730
 You know, what does the earth want?

00:29:18.870 --> 00:29:20.410
 What does the land want?

00:29:20.410 --> 00:29:23.220
 You know, and that's, that's important.

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:26.610
 So, Severin, what, why does it take people?

